<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nîmes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nemausus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nîmes is a city in the Gard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Founded around a sacred spring as Nemausus, the chief town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volcae">Volcae</a> Arecomici, it became a Roman colony under Augustus and keeps outstanding Roman monuments, including an amphitheatre, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maison-carree">Maison Carrée</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tour-magne">Tour Magne</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-trencavel">Trencavel</a> viscounts and the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nimes.jpg" alt="Nîmes" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shadowgate from Novara, ITALY · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nimes_2012_(8579722371).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
